{"context": "A research team was planning an explicit grant program to fund large-scale AI safety efforts and wanted to attract entrepreneurial applicants who might otherwise start purely commercial ventures. They had argued the program's scope should be broad enough to house promising teams regardless of institutional setting. They now needed to decide which concrete research areas the grants should target.\n\nQuestion: Which research directions should the team prioritize for the grant program?", "options": ["Prioritize safety monitoring as the grant program's lead research direction over other candidate areas, where scaling the work is expected to have high returns.", "Prioritize automating AI safety research itself across all technical areas, since automated labor is the fastest route to scaling safety work under short timelines."], "principles": [], "persona": "An AI safety researcher or technical forecaster who prizes epistemic rigor, precise conceptual distinctions, and methodological soundness. They insist on clear definitional boundaries—scheming vs. reward hacking, distinct task capabilities vs. single capability estimates—over tidy collapsible taxonomies. They favor theory-first reasoning (coverage claims need defined criteria) paired with concrete, mechanism-focused empirical evidence over speculative arguments. They are scope-disciplined: they prefer tractable, well-founded analyses over ambitious extensions built on noisy inputs, and conservative, hedged forecasts that flag model limitations over confident headline numbers. On alignment risk, they take deceptive alignment seriously as an incentive-structure concern while remaining cautious about unproven claims, favoring layered defenses (control, interpretability, oversight), mechanism-aware measurement, and mitigations that don't merely train models to hide the measured signal. They distrust lab-to-deployment extrapolation, preferring real-world data even if noisy. They are skeptical of low-evidence-value experiments with confounded interpretations, preferring controlled baselines, non-IID generalization tests, and diagnostics that cleanly distinguish hypotheses. They value conceptual generality (architecture-agnostic interpretability, universal learning dynamics) and calm, accessible public communication.", "past_choices": []}
